<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part V &mdash; Speculative Appendix &middot; the Prime Being</p>
  <h1>The Prime Plane</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Suppose the line that keeps climbing counts in fives. What clock do the beings sent
    back obey? An eight-state return preserves the Fibonacci pairing and falls behind. A four-state return
    restores the quarter-turn and moves ahead. The arithmetic is simple. The choice between the two is not.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The conjecture, before the calculation</p>
    <h2 id="t-meaning">What “base” has to mean</h2>
    <p class="lede">A numeral base cannot create a physical phase shift merely by changing how a number is
      written. For this speculation to say anything, <em>base</em> must mean the number of equally spaced
      phase states in one local cycle.</p>
    <p>On that definition the prime plane has five states and advances by one fifth-turn at every shared
      tick. The return plane is allowed two rival clocks: eight states, as in the Fibonacci and octave
      reading; or four states, as in the quarter-turn and grounding reading. Let the relative phase be
      measured as prime minus return:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \omega_b=\frac{2\pi}{b},\qquad
      \Delta\theta_{5\to b}=\omega_5-\omega_b
      =2\pi\!\left(\frac15-\frac1b\right)
    \]</p>
    <p>The sign now has content. A positive value means the prime plane advances farther on each tick, so
      the return plane lags. A negative value means the return plane advances farther, so it leads. This is
      a convention about orientation, not yet a force law. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading on this page: <span class="sci">&#9670; exact conditional
      mathematics</span> &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; speculative assignment</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; interpretation or vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The fork</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-models">Two Return Clocks</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Both alternatives are internally clean. They differ in the size of the shift, the
      time required to rephase, and &mdash; most importantly &mdash; which plane is ahead.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <p class="kicker">Model A &mdash; base 5 to base 8</p>
      <h3 id="t-five-eight">The return plane lags</h3>
      <p>The prime clock moves by \(72^\circ\); the eight-state return clock moves by \(45^\circ\).
        The prime plane therefore gains twenty-seven degrees per shared tick.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \Delta\theta_{5\to8}
        =\frac{2\pi}{5}-\frac{2\pi}{8}
        =\frac{3\pi}{20}=27^\circ
      \]</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        N_{5,8}=\operatorname{lcm}(5,8)=40
      \]</p>
      <p class="muted">First full rephasing: forty ticks. The prime plane completes eight turns while the
        return plane completes five.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Model B &mdash; base 5 to base 4</p>
      <h3 id="t-five-four">The return plane leads</h3>
      <p>The prime clock still moves by \(72^\circ\); the four-state return clock moves by
        \(90^\circ\). The sign reverses: the return plane gains eighteen degrees per shared tick.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \Delta\theta_{5\to4}
        =\frac{2\pi}{5}-\frac{2\pi}{4}
        =-\frac{\pi}{10}=-18^\circ
      \]</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        N_{5,4}=\operatorname{lcm}(5,4)=20
      \]</p>
      <p class="muted">First full rephasing: twenty ticks. The prime plane completes four turns while the
        return plane completes five.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

