<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Why eight is attractive</p>
    <h2 id="t-case-eight">Five and Eight Belong to One Fibonacci Sentence</h2>
    <p>Five and eight are consecutive Fibonacci numbers. Their ratio is already close to the golden limit
      governing the return depth:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac85=1.6\approx\varphi=1.618033\ldots
    \]</p>
    <p>The difference between the two phase counts is three, the preceding Fibonacci number. The relative
      turn can therefore be written as one small Fibonacci relation:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Delta\theta_{5\to8}
      =2\pi\frac{8-5}{5\cdot8}
      =2\pi\frac{3}{40}
    \]</p>
    <p>That is why the forty-tick closure has a distinctive exchange. At closure the five-state plane has
      made eight turns, and the eight-state plane has made five. Each plane returns carrying the other's
      count. Within a framework already built from Fibonacci branching, the swap is not arbitrary decoration;
      it is the strongest internal argument for the eight-state return. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Its narrative consequence also fits the existing Prime Being chapter. The climbing line advances
      farther on every common tick. The returners remain behind it by an accumulating angle, so their
      deposits form a lagging field. If that lag couples to the polarisation, it gives the chapter a geometry
      for its phrase “pulled from behind.” <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted"><strong>The weakness:</strong> Fibonacci adjacency does not establish a clock. The
      framework still owes a reason why a returned being should have eight distinguishable phase states,
      why the splice should synchronise the two planes, and how an angular lag becomes acceleration.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Why four is a real rival</p>
    <h2 id="t-case-four">Five and Four Put the Return in Quadrature</h2>
    <p>Four has a different claim. It is the first spatial grounding in the framework's integer sequence:
      four directions, the square, the tetrahedral count of faces, and the quarter-turn that puts two axes
      into quadrature. A returner may not preserve the Fibonacci clock of the ascent. It may be projected
      back onto the fourfold plane that makes location possible.</p>
    <p>The four-state clock advances by a right angle while the prime plane advances by a pentagonal angle.
      Their relative step is smaller than in the eight-state model but has the opposite sign:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \left|\Delta\theta_{5\to4}\right|=\frac{\pi}{10}=18^\circ,
      \qquad
      \operatorname{sgn}(\Delta\theta_{5\to4})=-1
    \]</p>
    <p>The shorter twenty-tick beat is not merely half the other period; it exchanges the cycle counts in a
      different order. At closure the prime plane has made four turns and the return plane five. The returned
      field is now <em>ahead</em> of the prime line under the same orientation convention.</p>
    <p>That changes the story. A phase lead does not naturally picture a depth pulling from behind. It is
      better suited to anticipatory feedback, resistance, braking, or a reversal of handedness at the seam.
      None follows automatically, but the base-four alternative forces the framework to state which kind of
      coupling it needs instead of treating every non-zero phase as equivalent. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted"><strong>The weakness:</strong> four is structurally abundant, which makes it easy to
      invoke after the fact. This model gives up the consecutive-Fibonacci relation and must explain why the
      returning branch changes clocks from a fivefold ascent to a fourfold ground.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A second meaning that must not be mixed with the first</p>
    <h2 id="t-scale-reading">If “Base” Means a Multiplicative Scale</h2>
    <p>The cyclic model above treats base five, eight or four as a count of phase states. There is another
      possible meaning: the prime plane may mark densities at \(5^n\), while the return plane marks them at
      \(b^m\). On that reading, alignment would require</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      5^n=b^m,\qquad b\in\{8,4\}
    \]</p>
    <p>There is no positive-integer solution for either rival. Every positive power of four or eight is a
      power of two; every positive power of five has only five as a prime factor. The two grids meet at one
      and never meet exactly again. Equivalently, both \(\log_8 5\) and \(\log_4 5\) are irrational.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>A logarithmic phase can still be defined:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Phi_b(n)=2\pi\left\{n\log_b5\right\},
      \qquad b\in\{8,4\}
    \]</p>
    <p>where the braces mean fractional part. This phase never repeats exactly; it winds densely around the
      circle. That is a very different cosmology from the forty- or twenty-tick beat. One gives periodic
      rephasing because the clocks have finitely many states. The other gives permanent quasiperiodicity
      because the density scales are multiplicatively incommensurate.</p>
    <p class="lede">The framework must choose which meaning of base it intends. It cannot claim the finite
      closure of the cyclic model and the non-closing drift of the scale model in the same argument.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What would distinguish them</p>
    <h2 id="t-tests">Three Edges the Speculation Volunteers</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>One &mdash; recurrence</h3>
      <p>A forty-tick reset favours the five-to-eight cyclic model. A twenty-tick reset favours five to
        four. No exact reset, together with continuing phase drift, favours the multiplicative-scale reading.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Two &mdash; handedness</h3>
      <p>Under one fixed orientation, base eight produces a lag and base four a lead. Any proposed coupling
        must preserve that sign. A model that predicts the same effect from both has discarded the only
        sharp difference between them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Three &mdash; the clock</h3>
      <p>The framework must identify a physical event that deserves to be called the shared tick. Without
        one, “forty” and “twenty” count marks on a diagram rather than events in a world.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The present verdict</h3>
      <p>No such recurrence, handed coupling or common clock has been measured. The two models are therefore
        alternatives inside the vision, not rival physical theories. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-ledger">Earned, Chosen, Refused</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Earned</dt>
      <dd>Given five- and eight-state clocks, the shift is \(+3\pi/20\), the return lags, and the joint
        period is forty. Given five and four, the shift is \(-\pi/10\), the return leads, and the joint
        period is twenty. The least-common-multiple calculation, sign reversal, and irrationality of the
        multiplicative logarithms are ordinary mathematics.</dd>
      <dt>Chosen</dt>
      <dd>That the Prime Being has a five-state clock; that a returner has either eight or four states; that
        both receive one common tick; that relative phase survives the splice; and that phase can couple to
        polarisation, motion or memory.</dd>
      <dt>Refused</dt>
      <dd>Calling a radix a mechanism. Treating Fibonacci adjacency or spatial quadrature as evidence by
        itself. Ignoring the reversed sign of the base-four shift. Mixing finite cyclic closure with
        non-repeating logarithmic drift. Claiming acceleration before a coupling equation exists.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing stance</p>
    <p>If the Prime Being must be pulled by what it leaves behind, five against eight is the cleaner internal
      fit: the returners lag, the Fibonacci language survives, and the beat closes only after forty ticks.
      If return means projection back into a grounded quarter-turn plane, five against four is the stronger
      rival: the beat closes at twenty and the returners lead, forcing a different account of feedback.</p>
    <p class="lede">The value of the alternative is not that it disproves the first picture. It reveals
      which parts of that picture were doing work.</p>
